Upstream:      OpenShell (github.com/ai-openshell/openshell):
               crates/openshell-server/src/persistence/azure_workload_identity.rs,
               postgres.rs
Upstreamable:  yes
How:           An issue and a PR against main: with sslcert/sslkey in
               database_url, a gateway that outlives its client certificate
               can no longer open Postgres connections (SQLx 0.9 caches the TLS
               connector in PgConnectOptions), and the readiness probe fails.
Written:       2026-11-25, against OpenShell v0.1.10 (SQLx 0.9.1)
Last checked:  2026-11-25: main builds plain pools with From<PgPool>, which
               never replaces the connect options
Remove when:   a release reloads the database URL's TLS files, or SQLx stops
               caching the connector across connections
Applied by:    packages/openshell/outputs.nix

Postgres: reread the database URL's TLS files every five minutes.
SQLx 0.9 builds the TLS connector, reading sslcert, sslkey and sslrootcert, on
the first connection a PgConnectOptions opens, and keeps it in that options
value. A pool built from a URL therefore presents the client certificate it
started with for as long as the process runs. Once that certificate expires,
Postgres refuses every new connection ("certificate has expired") while the
renewed one sits unread in the mounted Secret.

The plain (non-Azure) pools now keep their URL. On acquire, at most once per
interval, the URL is parsed again and installed with set_connect_options, so
the next connection builds a new connector from the files on disk. Open
connections are left alone. A URL that no longer parses keeps the current
options and logs a warning. Unit tests cover the interval and the parse
failure.


